# DumpTriage MCP server

Triage crash dumps from your coding agent (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex or any other MCP client). Upload a dump from your
disk, wait for the analysis and read the root-cause report without leaving the editor (website#4343).

It runs on your machine and talks to the [DumpTriage API](https://performanceinsights.ai/api-docs/) with your API key.
It streams dumps from disk, so a multi-GB kernel dump never passes through the agent's context.

## Set up

Create an API key in the portal (**Account → API keys**). It needs the full scope: an upload-only key cannot read
reports. You also need [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/), which runs the server without a separate install.

Claude Code:

```sh
claude mcp add dumptriage --env DUMPTRIAGE_API_KEY=dts_... -- \
  uvx --from "git+https://github.com/performanceinsights-ai/dumptriage-tools@v0.4.0#subdirectory=mcp" dumptriage-mcp
```

Any client that takes a JSON config, such as Cursor's `.cursor/mcp.json` or Claude Desktop:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "dumptriage": {
      "command": "uvx",
      "args": ["--from", "git+https://github.com/performanceinsights-ai/dumptriage-tools@v0.4.0#subdirectory=mcp", "dumptriage-mcp"],
      "env": { "DUMPTRIAGE_API_KEY": "dts_..." }
    }
  }
}
```

The `@v0.4.0` pins a release tag, so the code you run doesn't change under you. Use the
[latest release](https://github.com/performanceinsights-ai/dumptriage-tools/releases/latest)'s tag.

| Variable | |
|---|---|
| `DUMPTRIAGE_API_KEY` | Required. It stays in the MCP config and is never a tool argument, so it stays out of the model's context |
| `DUMPTRIAGE_API_URL` | Optional, for a self-hosted engine. Defaults to `https://api.performanceinsights.ai`. Must be https; plain http is allowed only to this machine |

## Tools

| Tool | What it does |
|---|---|
| `upload_dump` | Uploads a local dump; a relative path is taken from the project directory. It sends the file's SHA-256, so the API refuses a cut or changed transfer, and an idempotency key, so a retried call does not upload twice. It reports progress while the file streams |
| `get_dump` | Returns the dump's status. With `wait_seconds` (at most 900) it polls until the status is `delivered`, `escalated`, `failed` or `expired` |
| `get_report` | Returns the analysis report |
| `list_dumps` | Lists your dumps, filtered by state, reference or upload time, with cursor paging |
| `list_dump_groups` | Lists recurring crashes, grouped by signature: count, hosts, first and last seen |
| `upload_symbols` | Uploads a PDB, an ELF debug file or a dSYM for a module the report could not name |

No tool can delete a dump or change account settings; use the portal or the API for those.

**Report text is data.** A report quotes strings read out of the dump: module names, exception messages, command
lines. The server tells the agent that this text is data, not instructions. Still review what an agent does after it
reads a report from a dump you did not produce.

## Tests

```sh
cd mcp && uv run --no-project --python 3.13 --with pytest --with pytest-asyncio \
  --with "mcp>=2.1.1,<3.0.0" --with "httpx>=0.27,<1.0" python -m pytest tests -q
```

The tests run the real `MCPServer` through the SDK's in-process `Client`, against an API mocked with
`httpx.MockTransport`. They pin the route and arguments each tool sends, and how an API refusal reaches the agent.
